# THE SHARED-&T UB LEDGER — the standing facts that rule
# `type.shared-ref.write-through-derived-ub` rests on.
#
# The rule: given `r: &T` with T Freeze, the referent is immutable for r's
# lifetime and a write through a pointer DERIVED FROM `r` is undefined. The
# exception that makes it livable: a pointer VALUE loaded OUT of the referent
# carries its own provenance, is not derived from `r`, and may be written
# through — which is exactly what Rc::clone, Arc::clone, Weak::upgrade and
# Rc/Arc::downgrade do.
#
# `shared_ref_ub_lint.sh` holds this file in BOTH directions: an entry that
# appears, disappears, or changes count is RED either way. The number can only
# move by somebody editing this file on purpose, which is the whole point — a
# census that drifts on its own is a measurement nobody is reading.
#
# ⚠ WHY A CENSUS AND NOT AN ASSERTION. Neither the LAUNDER section nor the
# INTERIOR one can be decided by reading text. Whether a given laundering site is
# sound depends on the receiver, the pointee's Freeze-ness, and whether the write
# lands in the referent's own bytes or behind a loaded pointer — none of which is
# visible in the shape. So the lint does not judge; it makes the population
# impossible to change quietly, and leaves the judging to the reviewer who adds
# the line.
#
# ── SECTION `INTERIOR` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY declaration in ALL of stdlib carrying a DIRECT `UnsafeCell` field, plus
# the lang item itself. This is the population `type_is_freeze` detects BY
# CONSTRUCTION: it answers non-Freeze only by finding an UnsafeCell in the
# pointee's inline bytes (or by recognising the lang item by qualified name), so
# every other non-Freeze type in the tree is non-Freeze because it reaches one of
# these.
#
# ⚠ THIS SECTION USED TO BE CALLED `ATOMIC` AND IT USED TO BE WRONG. It listed
# the 12 `pub struct Atomic*` from stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos — the one file
# the reader looked at — and the lint's closing line printed "12 interior-
# mutability primitives, all UnsafeCell-wrapped", which reads as a total. It is
# 19. MEASURED: replace `data: UnsafeCell<T>` with `data: T` in Mutex<T> and
# RwLock<T> (stdlib/lcm/sync/sync.logos), and both become Freeze — every
# `&Mutex<T>` in the tree would earn noalias+readonly — and the lint exited 0
# and still printed "12 … all UnsafeCell-wrapped". A census pointed at one file
# certifies one file.
#
# THE THIRD COLUMN is an `; AXIS <id>` marker in
# tests/logos/ir/param_attrs_freeze_interior.check, or `-`. It is the joint
# between this census and the EMITTED ARTIFACT: a census held only against
# source text is still a claim about text. The axes cover the types the
# soundness comment in src/compiler/mlir_gen_fn.cpp NAMES — Mutex, RwLock, the
# Writ arena — plus the cell family and ONE representative atomic. The other
# eleven atomics are `-` DELIBERATELY, so nobody reads them as covered: twelve
# near-identical axes would be noise, and the row is what makes the population
# unable to move quietly, not the axis.
#
# ⚠ `&Mutex<T>` and `&RwLock<T>` have NO call site in the tree today — every
# method on them takes `self: *mut Mutex<T>`, which is Kind::Ptr and never gets
# the attribute bundle. Their axes are standing gates, not live coverage. The
# live one is `Writ`: 367 `&Writ`/`&mut Writ` parameter occurrences in stdlib.
#
# FORMAT: `<repo-relative path>\t<Name>\t<axis id | ->`  (TAB-separated)
INTERIOR
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicBool	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicI16	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicI32	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicI64	axis_atomic_i64
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicI8	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicIsize	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicPtr	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicU16	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicU32	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicU64	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicU8	-
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	AtomicUsize	-
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	Cell	axis_cell
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	OnceCell	axis_oncecell
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	RefCell	axis_refcell
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	UnsafeCell	axis_unsafecell
stdlib/lang/writ/container.logos	Writ	axis_writ
stdlib/lcm/sync/sync.logos	Mutex	axis_mutex
stdlib/lcm/sync/sync.logos	RwLock	axis_rwlock
#
# ── SECTION `TRANSITIVE` ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Types that are non-Freeze ONLY because the field recursion reaches a root
# above. `LazyCell<T>` is the reason this section exists: it is
# `{ cell: OnceCell<T>, init: fn() -> T }` and has no UnsafeCell of its own, so a
# root census alone never names it — and `axis_lazycell` in
# param_attrs_freeze_interior.check is what goes red if the recursion ever stops
# one level early, which is exactly the shape of the tuple-arm defect that WAS
# measured to hand out a wrong noalias.
#
# ⚠ COMPUTED WITH THE PREDICATE'S OWN INDIRECTION STOP. A field behind `*`, `&`
# or `fn(` does not infect its container, because `type_is_freeze` stops there.
# That is why `Rc` and `Arc` are correctly ABSENT while `ArcInner` is present:
# the refcount lives behind a pointer Arc LOADS, which is the whole loaded-
# pointer exception. A syntactic closure without the stop would list Rc, Arc and
# every handle type in the tree — the opposite of the truth, and it would drown
# the row that matters.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT DOES NOT MODEL: generics. `Vec<Cell<i64>>` is not reported infected
# by its element type. That happens to be correct (the elements live behind
# Vec's pointer) but the reader gets it right by never looking, so do not read
# this section as a decision procedure. The INTERIOR section is the load-bearing
# one; this is the reachability report.
#
# 2026-08-06 — `AnyBox` (logos.mem.any.anybox) APPEARS here: it owns a `Writ`
# by value, and `Writ` is an INTERIOR root (two UnsafeCell fields), so the field
# recursion reaches a root and `AnyBox` is correctly non-Freeze. REVIEWED and
# intended: an `AnyBox` mutates the arena it owns through a shared `&self` — that
# is the whole ownership design ("the Any owns the arena"), and a `&AnyBox`
# earning noalias+readonly would be exactly the wrong answer.
#
# FORMAT: `<repo-relative path>\t<Name>\t<axis id | ->`  (TAB-separated)
TRANSITIVE
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	LazyCell	axis_lazycell
stdlib/lang/fabric/fabric.logos	Buffer	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/bt/cow.logos	LeafDirNode	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/bt/cow.logos	LeafNode	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/bt/cow.logos	LeafNodeForBranch	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/handle.logos	Snap	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/handle.logos	Store	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/data/store.logos	LockingStore	-
stdlib/lcm/deem/facthistory.logos	FactHistory	-
stdlib/mem/any/anybox.logos	AnyBox	-
stdlib/mem/bt/memstore.logos	SnapDesc	-
stdlib/mem/deem/eval.logos	Tpl	-
stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos	FactStore	-
stdlib/mem/deem/incr.logos	IncrJoin	-
stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos	IncrRec	-
stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos	QRows	-
stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos	Query	-
stdlib/mem/sync/arc.logos	ArcInner	-
stdlib/std/io/bytes/bytes.logos	BytesInner	-
#
# ── SECTION `LAUNDER` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-file count of the const->mut laundering shape in stdlib — the shape the
# rule declares undefined to write through.
#
# PATTERN: `as \*const [^;]* as \*mut ` on `//`-comment-stripped lines. It is
# DELIBERATELY BROADER than "same type on both sides": it also catches
# `as *const A as *mut B`, which launders just as well. Over-count, never
# under-count. Comments are stripped because this rule is the most heavily
# documented thing in the tree and a site that EXPLAINS the class must not read
# as an instance of it — otherwise the incentive becomes "document less".
#
# ⚠ WHAT A ROW DOES NOT MEAN. A row is not an accusation. Every site here was
# read and is sound for one of five reasons, none of them textual: a `&mut self`
# receiver (fabric push/set/insert/erase), a by-value `self` (Vec::into_iter,
# PrimVec::drop), a non-Freeze pointee (every writ site — Writ holds UnsafeCell),
# a cast used only to reach a `*mut`-receiver method for a READ (http types /
# serialize / parse, hashmap_at, deem/incr, node_arc_data_ptr), or THE
# LOADED-POINTER EXCEPTION (rc.logos, arc.logos — the refcount lives behind
# `self.inner`, which is loaded, so the write is not derived from the shared
# reference). That audit is a hand read of one syntactic form: it is evidence,
# not a proof of absence, which is precisely why the population is pinned here
# rather than re-derived by hand the next time somebody asks.
#
# ⚠ AND WHAT THIS SECTION CANNOT DO: it says nothing about USER code. Nothing in
# the tree detects the prohibited shape outside stdlib; a real detector must
# model the provenance chain, because a syntactic matcher would fire on every
# Rc/Arc/Weak method above. That is an open question, not an oversight.
#
# 2026-08-06 — the writ deep-copy walker MOVED, so one row moved with it. The
# recursive walk that lived in `stdlib/mem/writ/clone.logos` (a second, drifted
# copy of `logos.lang.writ.compactify`) was folded into the lang one and that
# file became a two-line delegator; its single `dst as *const Writ as *mut Writ`
# went with it. REVIEWED: same site, same reason as every other writ row — the
# pointee is `Writ`, which holds `UnsafeCell`, so it is non-Freeze and a `&Writ`
# is neither noalias nor readonly. The cast reaches `writ_alloc_bytes`, whose
# receiver is `*mut Writ` purely to read `.allocator`; the arena mutation goes
# through the `UnsafeCell` as it always did. Net stdlib count is unchanged (1
# added under lang/writ, 1 removed under mem/writ) — the walk was not duplicated.
#
# FORMAT: `<repo-relative path>\t<count>`  (TAB-separated)
LAUNDER
stdlib/lang/atomic/atomic.logos	2
stdlib/lang/cell/cell.logos	2
stdlib/lang/fabric/fabric.logos	6
